description.narrative:
Africa is facing environmental challenges of unprecedented magnitude that significantly affect terrestrial and marine ecosystems. These challenges include biodiversity loss, deterioration of marine and coastal ecosystems, as well as threats to protected areas. These environmental issues are exacerbated by factors such as climate change, overexploitation of natural resources and deforestation.
Faced with these challenges, it is imperative to adopt an integrated approach, combining nature conservation and improving the living conditions of local communities. It is with this in mind that ecosystem restoration is emerging as a strategic response to these major environmental issues, while providing solutions for mitigation and adaptation to climate change.
The Biosphere Reserve (BR) model allows multisectoral and multidisciplinary coordination at the scale of a landscape to demonstrate the potential of this integrated approach.
The project will focus on an existing BR, Chimanimani in Zimbabwe, that was established in 2021; and a potential BR in Botswana, the Makgadikgadi landscape, that was selected by the Botswana MAB Committee as priority for nomination. The project will be implemented in parallel with a complementary project that will be implemented by the Fondation OCP and Université Mohamed VI Polytechnique.
The overall objective of this project is to contribute to the restoration of ecosystems, while strengthening the resilience of local populations and improving their living conditions. To achieve this objective, the program is structured around the following distinct areas of intervention:
- Institutional support and capacity building;
- Support for local communities;
- Improvement of the conservation status of biodiversity;
- Awareness-raising, exchange of experiences and advocacy;
- Mapping Indigenous knowledge
